{{also|frog-march|frog march}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|frog march}}, {{l|en|frog-march}}, {{l|en|frog's march}}

===Etymology===
See {{m|en|frog march}}.

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To march or force a person forward while holding their arms from behind or the side, as a prisoner.
#* '''2024''', Ryan Lenora Brown, ''[https://www.csmonitor.com/World/Africa/2024/0529/south-africa-ANC-loses-election-popularity South Africans head to polls. After 30 years, has Mandela’s party lost its luster?]'', in: The Christian Science Monitor, May 29 2024
#*: Maybe it happened during the COVID-19 lockdowns, when no one could work and soldiers '''frog-marched''' curfew-breakers through the township streets like criminals.
# {{lb|en|figurative}} To force a person forward against their will.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1940|author=w:Thomas Firbank|title=I Bought a Mountain| passage=The wind '''frogmarched''' me at a run into the house.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} To carry a person [[face-down]] with one person holding each limb.
# To [[forcibly]] [[relocate]] a person, especially in a [[degrading]] or [[humiliating]] manner.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The process of frogmarching a person.

